0	105	The Barroso Commission was the European Commission in office from 22 November 2004 until 31 October 2014.
106	291	Its president was José Durão Barroso, who presided over 27 other commissioners (one from each of the states composing the European Union, aside from Portugal, which is Barroso's state).
292	455	On 16 September 2009 Barroso was re-elected by the European Parliament for a further five years and his Commission was approved to take office on 9 February 2010.

455	704	Barroso was at first seen as the lowest common denominator by outside commentators, but his proposed team of Commissioners earned him some respect before triggering a crisis when the European Parliame
705	804	In 2007 the Commission gained two new members when Romania and Bulgaria joined the European Union.

804	890	Barroso's handling of his office was markedly more presidential than his predecessors.
891	995	During his term the Commission passed major legislation including the REACH and 'Bolkestein' Directives.
996	1083	Under Barroso, the civil service in the Commission became more economically liberal.



1083	1167	History

Barroso was nominated as president and approved by Parliament in July 2004.
1168	1413	However his proposed Commission met with opposition from the Parliament, notably concerning Rocco Buttiglione and his conservative comments which were seen as incompatible with his role as European Co
1414	1604	The opposition plunged the EU into a minor crisis before Barroso conceded to the Parliament and reshuffled his team, removing Buttiglione, and his Commission took office on 22 November 2004.
1605	1711	The commission was joined in 2007 by two further Commissioners when Bulgaria and Romania joined the EU.



