27615	27703	Civil service

The Secretary-General for this term was Catherine Day, appointed in 2005.
27704	27781	She was the first woman to hold the post and took over from David O'Sullivan.
27782	28034	Early favourite François Lamoureux, previously in charge of Transport, had health problems and his being infirm was seen as a political blow to France and a final break from the Delors era, instead gi
28035	28139	Commissioner Kallas stressed the appointments were on merit, resisting pressure from national capitals.

28139	28236	Day was part of a vast reshuffle of Commission officials by the President after he came to power.
28237	28375	The reshuffle was welcomed by the right-wing as the new directors-general, including Day, were liberal reformers, many British and German.
28376	28520	A move which did not go down well with France, symbolic of their loss of influence, who were opposed to Day due to her liberal economic reforms.
28521	28620	Indeed, Day became very notable in the commission for her opposition to French state aid policies.

28620	28838	Concern has been expressed by Commissioners such as Günter Verheugen in regards to overlap of mandates and fractionalisation of the commission's Directorates-General, leading to "turf-wars" and a lack
28839	29027	A great deal of the commission's effort is expended in this in-fighting and weaker Commissioners, such as Verheugen, have not been able to gain sufficient control over their department.



29027	29148	Politicisation

During Barroso's tenure, the commission has seen a general increase in the politicisation of its members.
29149	29293	Although members are supposed to remain above national politics, members have been involved in national elections or backed national candidates.
29294	29526	For example, Commissioner Michel participated in the 2007 Belgian elections while Commissioner Kroes backed Angela Merkel in the 2005 German elections and vice-president Wallström backed Ségolène Roya
29526	29773	Michel claimed that politicisation of this manner is part of reconnecting the Union with its citizens and Wallström defended it claiming that the EU has to get more political and controversial as bein
29774	29966	Wallström presented plans to give greater prominence to European political parties ahead of the 2009 elections and give the parties the ability to run with candidates for Commission President.
29967	30098	There was speculation that under this scenario, if he gained backing, Barroso might run as the People's Party candidate in 2009.



